A tiny experimental PostgreSQL extension to try to emulate an ancient broken
locale in a dead version of Debian/Ubuntu's distribution of glibc.

Though its /usr/share/i18n/locales/C file *looked* OK (it listed every
codepoint, in order), it doesn't seem to work, either on old glibc or if
recompiled with modern localedef + glibc.

all_codepoints_ubuntu1804-C.UTF8.txt was captured from Ubuntu 18.04's
/usr/bin/sort given the output print_all_codepoints.py, with
LANG and LC_COLLATE set to C.UTF-8.  It is used to build a lookup table in this
module, to reproduce it faithfully if you use C.UTF-8 as the database default
locale.  The function to do that is injected into PostgreSQL's locale system at
session start through dubious means.

That wouldn't work in general due to the weights and rules used in "real"
locales.  But this is a simple character-at-a-time ordering, so observing the
order of all possible codepoints should be sufficient to reproduce it, I think?

To try it, "make install" with pg_config visible in your PATH, and put this in
your postgresql.conf:

  session_preload_libraries='emulate_old_ubuntu_c_utf8_strcoll.so'

Note that modern glibc has proper built-in support for C.UTF-8, and sorts by
codepoint order correctly.

